15
00:00:41,645 --> 00:00:48,020

16
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:48,520
Great.

17
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,140
Thanks for having me.

18
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,750
So the title of my
talk today is recipes

19
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:56,050
for training helpful chatbots.

20
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:58,030
So here's the introduction.

21
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:01,310
I was part of this team called
the H4 for at Hugging Face.

22
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:03,490
And today, I'll walk
you through what

23
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:08,190
we've built, how we decided on
what we need for building that.

24
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:09,940
And so essentially
what we wanted to build

25
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:12,010
and the goal of the
team and the project

26
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:16,660
since earlier this year was to
figure out a recipe for H4 which

27
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:19,210
stands for helpful,
harmless, honest, and Huggy,

28
00:01:19,210 --> 00:01:22,060
because it's Hugging
Face, chatbot.

29
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:24,520
And so like, you know, the
ingredients essentially

30
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:27,340
were to figure out
what kind of data sets

31
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:30,910
do we need for supervised
fine-tuning and RLHF,

32
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:34,720
and we wanted to like, you know,
not worry about pre-training,

33
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:37,270
instead take an open
source pre-trained model

34
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:40,690
and recreate the secret
sauce of alignment on it.

35
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:43,660
And the procedure that we
wanted to follow and replicate

36
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:47,260
on open source is this figure
that I'm pretty sure most of you

37
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:49,070
are familiar with
at this point, it's

38
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:52,340
like from this InstructGPT
paper from OpenAI,

39
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:54,710
which shows three steps.

40
00:01:54,710 --> 00:01:57,980
I'm going to go into a
bit more detail on this

41
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:00,380
because this slide
is much smaller here,

42
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:03,260
but this is what the outline
of the talk looks like.

43
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,970
I'll be getting
the detail of like,

44
00:02:04,970 --> 00:02:07,640
you know, how did we decide what
kind of data, how much data,

45
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:11,360
and all the details of the data
for supervised fine-tuning,

46
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,330
then similarly
for RLHF, then I'm

47
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:18,440
going to talk about distillation
of language model alignment,

48
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:21,980
then experiments with
different helpfulness recipes,

49
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:25,040
finaly talk about
evaluation of these models,

50
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:29,405
and quirks of using
GPT-4 as an evaluator.

51
00:02:29,405 --> 00:02:29,905
OK.

52
00:02:29,905 --> 00:02:31,450
So this is kind
of like, you know,

53
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:35,500
what overall recipe that
InstructGPT paper from OpenAI

54
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,820
put forward as the steps
for training a chatbot.

55
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:43,390
So the first step over here is
to do supervised fine-tuning.

56
00:02:43,390 --> 00:02:47,230
Essentially you're doing fine
tuning with human instruction

57
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:50,020
demonstration data, so
the input and the output

58
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:51,630
are both given by humans.

59
00:02:51,630 --> 00:02:55,270
The step two is the input
is given by a human,

60
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:58,030
the output comes from
models, and then the human

61
00:02:58,030 --> 00:03:00,628
just reads thumbs up,
thumbs down or ranks them.

62
00:03:00,628 --> 00:03:02,920
And then you train a reward
model, which is essentially

63
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:04,190
just a classifier.

64
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:06,700
And then the final step
three is doing fine-tuning

65
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,600
using that reward model
with reinforcement learning.

66
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:13,300
And so the way I'm looking
at is like step one is

67
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:16,570
more for making a
model into a helpful

68
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:19,360
chatbot, and the steps
two and are essentially

69
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:23,060
trying to add those guardrails
in place for harmlessness

70
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:26,360
So let's get started with
talking about helpfulness,

71
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:31,370
and most of my talk today will
be focused on the step one.

72
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:35,060
So let's start diving
deeper into this,

73
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:36,860
and let's start out
with the dataset.

74
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:39,950
Like, how do we decide
what we need for doing

75
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:41,930
the supervised fine-tuning?

76
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:45,620
So the dataset for helpfulness
for supervised fine-tuning

77
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:47,490
looks somewhat like this.

78
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:49,550
This is from the
self-instruct paper.

79
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:51,990
If you are aware of that
from end of last year,

80
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:53,600
so you have something
that we call

81
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:57,350
as a task, which then has an
instruction which is essentially

82
00:03:57,350 --> 00:04:01,700
a request by a user asking
the model to fulfill or give

83
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:03,210
a response to a certain task.

84
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:06,940
And that is followed by
an input and an output.

85
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:08,970
The input in this
case is optional.

86
00:04:08,970 --> 00:04:11,420
It could just be part
of the instruction,

87
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,910
and then the output
is the expected output

88
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:15,390
that the model should generate.

89
00:04:15,390 --> 00:04:17,390
But while we are
doing this training,

90
00:04:17,390 --> 00:04:19,610
the human provides
the expected output

91
00:04:19,610 --> 00:04:22,670
that the model would have
generated in the actual test

92
00:04:22,670 --> 00:04:23,690
case.

93
00:04:23,690 --> 00:04:25,730
And so here the
input and the output

94
00:04:25,730 --> 00:04:29,070
are called instance or
demonstration or completion,

95
00:04:29,070 --> 00:04:34,280
and that's why this is called
instruction demonstration.

96
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:37,190
So this is just a
high level landscape

97
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:40,550
of what these datasets for
instruction demonstration

98
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:44,420
look like, and you must have
been familiar with at least some

99
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:45,560
of these.

100
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:50,000
And the way I've tried to put
this is on this line where

101
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,600
on one side, I'm showing
datasets that were generated

102
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,760
using models or more
powerful language models,

103
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:58,920
and so they're more
synthetic data sets.

104
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:02,070
On the right, I'm showing
like human written datasets,

105
00:05:02,070 --> 00:05:07,400
and so these are datasets that
the human wrote the input as

106
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:09,180
well as the expected output.

107
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:10,820
And so examples
of these are like,

108
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,290
you know, so the
surge-instruct is

109
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:15,620
the data set that we at
Hugging Face H4, you know,

110
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:18,530
contracted with this
company that, you know,

111
00:05:18,530 --> 00:05:22,070
that basically had contracts
with annotators that

112
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:23,670
were writing the
inputs and outputs,

113
00:05:23,670 --> 00:05:26,540
but we had to give them all
the specifications of what

114
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:28,100
kind of data we need.

115
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:29,810
And then you must
have heard of--

116
00:05:29,810 --> 00:05:32,690
obviously, Open Assistant is
this other community wide effort

117
00:05:32,690 --> 00:05:34,700
where people
contributed manually

118
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:37,910
writing inputs and outputs,
similarly with Dolly.

119
00:05:37,910 --> 00:05:39,530
And then on the
other hand, you can

120
00:05:39,530 --> 00:05:41,570
see the self-instruct
instruct dataset.

121
00:05:41,570 --> 00:05:43,590
I'm going to dive
into some of these.

122
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,050
How are these
synthetic data sets

123
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:50,960
created for helpfulness or
for supervised fine tuning?

124
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:54,530
So one of the examples of how
the synthetic data is created

125
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:56,750
is in the self construct
paper, which is

126
00:05:56,750 --> 00:05:58,350
called bootstrapping the data.

127
00:05:58,350 --> 00:06:03,860
So in this case, they start with
175 seat task that is a 175--

128
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:06,800
like a very small
dataset of examples

129
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:10,160
where the manually written
inputs and outputs from humans,

130
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:13,880
those are added to a task
pool, then a language model--

131
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:16,865
basically, you
bootstrap by giving that

132
00:06:16,865 --> 00:06:18,740
to the language model
and a few shot setting,

133
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:22,250
and ask it to generate
more data like that.

134
00:06:22,250 --> 00:06:24,200
And then you have another
language model that

135
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:26,250
does this task classification.

136
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:28,400
Like, you know, what
kind of task is this?

137
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:31,530
Is this sample or the
example are belonging to?

138
00:06:31,530 --> 00:06:34,940
And finally, it also does this
more fine grained classification

139
00:06:34,940 --> 00:06:37,910
as to like, you know, does it
have, you know, output first

140
00:06:37,910 --> 00:06:40,080
or does it require
input first and so on.

141
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:41,810
And because this
is synthetic data

142
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:43,910
and created in this like
a very scalable way,

143
00:06:43,910 --> 00:06:46,850
you also have to do a lot
of filtering to make sure

144
00:06:46,850 --> 00:06:50,430
that it is very high quality.

145
00:06:50,430 --> 00:06:53,760
So another way of generating
this kind of synthetic data

146
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:55,600
is what UltraChat did.

147
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:58,960
And in this case, they had like
a human in the loop process.

148
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:02,850
So a human would look up
like either, you know,

149
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:07,530
search Wikipedia or something,
and then come up with topics

150
00:07:07,530 --> 00:07:11,370
that they want to generate data
for, and then ask the model,

151
00:07:11,370 --> 00:07:14,310
like, provide it with the
required material that

152
00:07:14,310 --> 00:07:17,820
would be needed for coming up
with, say, question answering

153
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:20,670
or summarization or any
of these specific tasks,

154
00:07:20,670 --> 00:07:23,340
and then give it to a more
powerful model like ChatGPT

155
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:24,270
or GPT-4.

156
00:07:24,270 --> 00:07:26,640
In this case, it was
ChatGPT and then--

157
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:29,460
actually, GPT-4,
and then you keep

158
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:32,910
doing these loops of giving
the material to the model

159
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:35,130
and say like, come up
with questions and answers

160
00:07:35,130 --> 00:07:37,870
of on this particular task
using all this material.

161
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:40,800
And then the human
looks at it, and then

162
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,270
keeps querying it and
refining it more and more.

163
00:07:43,270 --> 00:07:45,770
So this is another way of
creating synthetic data.

164
00:07:45,770 --> 00:07:47,640
Obviously, this has
a human sitting there

165
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:50,890
and doing a lot more
filtering in the process.

166
00:07:50,890 --> 00:07:52,690
Then there's
another one which is

167
00:07:52,690 --> 00:07:55,580
like even less human involved,
which is roleplaying,

168
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:57,970
And this is the CAMEL dataset.

169
00:07:57,970 --> 00:08:00,790
In this case, all
that the human does

170
00:08:00,790 --> 00:08:06,200
is come up with an idea of what
task or what example they want.

171
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:08,740
So at a high level, it would
be like develop a trading

172
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:12,110
bot for the stock market,
and there would be two LLMs.

173
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:15,310
One would be role playing
as an AI assistant,

174
00:08:15,310 --> 00:08:18,020
the other would be
role playing as a user,

175
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,590
and then they basically
just specify the task

176
00:08:20,590 --> 00:08:23,290
and let these two bots
chat with each other

177
00:08:23,290 --> 00:08:25,450
and create a
conversation dataset,

178
00:08:25,450 --> 00:08:31,910
which is like a synthetic data
set for supervised fine-tuning.

179
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:35,630
So this is just going
back to this landscape,

180
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:38,669
it looks like, you know, there
people have been very creative,

181
00:08:38,669 --> 00:08:41,330
and how do we get
very high quality data

182
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:43,730
quickly without
spending a lot of money.

183
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:46,732
And because humans are
inefficient and expensive,

184
00:08:46,732 --> 00:08:49,440
and so these are like, you know,
some examples that we looked at.

185
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:53,900
But on the other hand, we also
cannot underestimate how good

186
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:56,670
quality the manually
created datasets are.

187
00:08:56,670 --> 00:09:00,140
And so we at
Hugging Face decided

188
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:02,390
to go with everything
very manual

189
00:09:02,390 --> 00:09:05,280
and have humans do both
the input and the output.

190
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:09,140
Also go figure out what
are the essential documents

191
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:11,960
or other material they
need for coming up

192
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:14,570
with creating this data.

193
00:09:14,570 --> 00:09:17,580
But when we started doing that,
we were earlier in the year,

194
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:20,240
so this is back in January
or February of this year,

195
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:23,300
and this is what the landscape
looked like at that time.

196
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:26,190
And so there was very
little datasets available.

197
00:09:26,190 --> 00:09:29,570
A lot of these were mostly
synthetically created.

198
00:09:29,570 --> 00:09:33,550
So we wanted to kind of leverage
what was existing out there,

199
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:36,270
but we also had to make some
really important decisions

200
00:09:36,270 --> 00:09:38,400
because we're going to
pay money and make sure

201
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:41,820
that the data that we collect
is actually useful for building

202
00:09:41,820 --> 00:09:45,970
the model and the applications
that are built on top of it.

203
00:09:45,970 --> 00:09:49,240
So these are the learnings that
we had from the past papers

204
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:52,550
that were creating these
supervised fine-tuning datasets.

205
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:54,670
We knew that the
dataset has to be

206
00:09:54,670 --> 00:09:57,560
in the range of tens of
thousands of examples.

207
00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:59,920
So this is from the
self-instruct dataset.

208
00:09:59,920 --> 00:10:03,640
And we also knew that
these models that

209
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:06,430
are trained on this data set
showed diminishing returns

210
00:10:06,430 --> 00:10:09,470
after just a few thousand
high quality instructions.

211
00:10:09,470 --> 00:10:12,688
So you don't need a lot, and
then it saturates very quickly.

212
00:10:12,688 --> 00:10:14,230
So these are the
two findings that we

213
00:10:14,230 --> 00:10:16,670
had when we started
to go collect data

214
00:10:16,670 --> 00:10:20,000
set for supervised fine-tuning.

215
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,750
But we also had to give some
very fine grained specifications

216
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:25,880
on what we want
for our data set.

217
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:27,530
In particular, we
had to decide what

218
00:10:27,530 --> 00:10:29,720
is the task distribution
we want for the data

219
00:10:29,720 --> 00:10:30,870
that we are collecting.

220
00:10:30,870 --> 00:10:32,495
I mean, we know it's
tens of thousands,

221
00:10:32,495 --> 00:10:35,360
but how many thousands
of what task, right?

222
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:37,190
The length distribution,
like, you know,

223
00:10:37,190 --> 00:10:39,240
should the prompt
have a certain length?

224
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:41,660
Is that even an
important factor?

225
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:43,460
And one thing is
that we wanted we

226
00:10:43,460 --> 00:10:45,110
had decided that
we want to make it

227
00:10:45,110 --> 00:10:47,330
high quality and human
written, but then there

228
00:10:47,330 --> 00:10:49,230
were like options
on that as well.

229
00:10:49,230 --> 00:10:50,960
We could go with
external vendors

230
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:54,830
like Surge, Scale AI, AWS
Ground Truth, and so on,

231
00:10:54,830 --> 00:10:56,840
or we could hire
our own contractors

232
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:58,440
from Upwork and MTurk.

233
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:01,260
And so those were decisions
that we had to make.

234
00:11:01,260 --> 00:11:04,550
So let's look at each
of these one by one.

235
00:11:04,550 --> 00:11:08,720
So because we were recreating
this InstructGPT recipe

236
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:12,350
for this helpful chatbot, we
wanted to take inspiration

237
00:11:12,350 --> 00:11:13,950
from their task distribution.

238
00:11:13,950 --> 00:11:16,700
So on the left, I'm showing
the task distribution

239
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:19,010
that InstructGPT did for--

240
00:11:19,010 --> 00:11:21,020
OpenAI did for the
InstructGPT paper.

241
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:24,530
As you can see, that generation
is the majority of it,

242
00:11:24,530 --> 00:11:27,740
followed by some of
these open ended tasks

243
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:29,520
and brainstorming
tasks and so on.

244
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:32,720
And these are examples of what
prompts of each of those look

245
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:33,830
like.

