00:14:33,720 --> 00:14:37,183
started looking at more
carefully after we had already

311
00:14:37,183 --> 00:14:38,100
collected the dataset.

312
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:41,300

313
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:44,000
So here are examples
of what that dataset

314
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,850
looked like, you
know, classification,

315
00:14:46,850 --> 00:14:48,335
generation, brainstorming.

316
00:14:48,335 --> 00:14:50,210
I'm sure you all must
have seen at least some

317
00:14:50,210 --> 00:14:53,300
of these kind of examples of
instruction, demonstration

318
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:53,880
datasets.

319
00:14:53,880 --> 00:14:56,120
So it's very much
like it has everything

320
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:59,270
that you can expect
from NLP kind of tasks,

321
00:14:59,270 --> 00:15:03,370
but also more open ended
chatty tasks as well.

322
00:15:03,370 --> 00:15:08,150

323
00:15:08,150 --> 00:15:11,960
OK, so here are some
details about the task force

324
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:15,530
that was used by Surge
to generate this dataset.

325
00:15:15,530 --> 00:15:18,260
We requested a US-based
based task force mainly

326
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:21,680
because, like I said, we
just wanted to replicate what

327
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:25,040
InstructGPT was doing, and based
on Anthropic and OpenAI's paper,

328
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:28,100
it seemed like they prefer going
with the US-based based task

329
00:15:28,100 --> 00:15:29,150
force.

330
00:15:29,150 --> 00:15:32,840
The gender, roughly equally
divided, and the age range

331
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:36,860
was also very, you know, very
it was like a big range going

332
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:39,230
all the way from 19 to 62.

333
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:42,830
And then people had
education background ranges

334
00:15:42,830 --> 00:15:44,460
from technical degree to PhD.

335
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:47,820
So PhD was mainly for tasks
like math, coding, and so on.

336
00:15:47,820 --> 00:15:50,760

337
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:54,150
OK, so now I wanted to
switch gears a little bit,

338
00:15:54,150 --> 00:15:56,310
and talk about
this, this data set

339
00:15:56,310 --> 00:16:00,430
that we collected for RLHF
or for human preferences

340
00:16:00,430 --> 00:16:02,910
before I get into
the experiments

341
00:16:02,910 --> 00:16:05,220
we ran with this supervised
fine-tuning data set

342
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:07,920
and what results we got.

343
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:11,160
So again, over here, while we
were collecting human preference

344
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:13,590
data set, we had to
come up with what

345
00:16:13,590 --> 00:16:15,820
are the specifications
of these data sets.

346
00:16:15,820 --> 00:16:18,570
So again, just to contrast
this with how is it

347
00:16:18,570 --> 00:16:22,050
different from SFT, the SFT
data set, both the input

348
00:16:22,050 --> 00:16:24,060
and the output are
written by humans.

349
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:26,430
In this case, the human
rights, the input,

350
00:16:26,430 --> 00:16:28,950
the output comes from
models, which is responses,

351
00:16:28,950 --> 00:16:34,920
but then the human just ranks or
rates them on a certain scale.

352
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:36,852
So yeah, essentially
we had to decide

353
00:16:36,852 --> 00:16:38,310
like, what is the
task distribution

354
00:16:38,310 --> 00:16:40,980
looks like for RLHF data?

355
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:43,920
Is it going to be same as
supervised fine-tuning?

356
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:45,870
What about the
length distribution?

357
00:16:45,870 --> 00:16:48,700
And should we do like single
turn versus multi-turn?

358
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:51,520
So in InstructGPT it
was mainly single turn.

359
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:53,548
So if we are trying to
replicate InstructGPT,

360
00:16:53,548 --> 00:16:55,090
we would have to go
with single turn,

361
00:16:55,090 --> 00:16:57,000
but if we are trying
to replicate something

362
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:01,170
like ChatGPT, it would have to
be like a multi-turn dialogue.

363
00:17:01,170 --> 00:17:02,640
And then we had
to also like, you

364
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:06,810
know, decide on these dimensions
of like helpfulness, honesty

365
00:17:06,810 --> 00:17:07,599
and harmlessness.

366
00:17:07,599 --> 00:17:09,810
So these are like the HHH
that Anthropic follows,

367
00:17:09,810 --> 00:17:13,319
like OpenAI puts it as like
helpfulness, truthfulness

368
00:17:13,319 --> 00:17:14,463
and harmlessness.

369
00:17:14,463 --> 00:17:16,380
And then also we had to
decide like, you know,

370
00:17:16,380 --> 00:17:18,869
are they going to rate
each of the responses

371
00:17:18,869 --> 00:17:21,040
individually or are
they going to rank them?

372
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:23,280
And what are the
implications of us

373
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:26,970
deciding one way or the other?

374
00:17:26,970 --> 00:17:30,760
So we started by doing
a pilot study again.

375
00:17:30,760 --> 00:17:34,570
So we took 300 prompts from
the self-instruct dataset,

376
00:17:34,570 --> 00:17:37,260
the data set that was
released end of last year,

377
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:41,820
and then gave it a generated
model responses from our models,

378
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:44,610
and then gave it to
data vendors to rate

379
00:17:44,610 --> 00:17:46,120
the responses of the models.

380
00:17:46,120 --> 00:17:48,120
And we used this
Anthropic template

381
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:51,000
on the left, which
is essentially

382
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:52,740
asking the human
to choose the most

383
00:17:52,740 --> 00:17:54,310
helpful and honest response.

384
00:17:54,310 --> 00:17:57,510
And then these are the responses
from model A and model B,

385
00:17:57,510 --> 00:18:00,240
and this is a
scale, which is also

386
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:02,850
working as sort of a
ranking thing in the sense

387
00:18:02,850 --> 00:18:06,600
that one to four is like
decreasingly model A,

388
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:12,020
and five to eight is
increasingly model B.

389
00:18:12,020 --> 00:18:15,020
And also one other thing
we had to decide about

390
00:18:15,020 --> 00:18:17,780
is how much data
should we collect.

391
00:18:17,780 --> 00:18:20,640
And so again, this is from
the InstructGPT paper.

392
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:24,170
And as you can see, they
have the train and validation

393
00:18:24,170 --> 00:18:26,150
splits for each of
the three steps, which

394
00:18:26,150 --> 00:18:29,600
are the SFD, training the
reward model, and the PPO.

395
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:32,310
And this one is in the
order of tens of thousands,

396
00:18:32,310 --> 00:18:35,150
and like over all this combined,
which is like, you know,

397
00:18:35,150 --> 00:18:43,130
this process of RLHF,
comes up to about 100,000.

398
00:18:43,130 --> 00:18:43,630
Great.

399
00:18:43,630 --> 00:18:44,130
OK.

400
00:18:44,130 --> 00:18:48,460
So then once we got this pilot
study data back, we sat down

401
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:50,170
and we wanted to also--

402
00:18:50,170 --> 00:18:51,910
so I looked at it
manually, and I

403
00:18:51,910 --> 00:18:54,910
felt that I did not agree
with most of the answers

404
00:18:54,910 --> 00:18:59,090
that the annotators from each of
these companies were providing.

405
00:18:59,090 --> 00:19:00,880
And so I was kind
of like, you know,

406
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:03,393
I don't think this is
high quality at all.

407
00:19:03,393 --> 00:19:05,560
So what I decided is like,
you know, I told my team,

408
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:08,240
let's go and rate
it within ourselves.

409
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:13,120
And then, we basically rated
about 100 examples or so,

410
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:16,310
and we followed a similar
template of one to four and five

411
00:19:16,310 --> 00:19:17,800
to eight.

412
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:20,200
Basically, the output,
like, you know, the takeaway

413
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:22,880
was that even we did not
agree amongst each other.

414
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:26,590
So essentially, like our models
earlier in the year were so bad,

415
00:19:26,590 --> 00:19:29,470
you were essentially breaking
ties like arbitrarily.

416
00:19:29,470 --> 00:19:32,320
Like, you know, you're deciding
between like, should it be like,

417
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:35,120
you know, three versus seven
or something like that.

418
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:37,180
So if they're equally
bad, it's hard to decide

419
00:19:37,180 --> 00:19:38,810
which one is better, right?

420
00:19:38,810 --> 00:19:42,050
And so we were breaking some
of these ties arbitrarily.

421
00:19:42,050 --> 00:19:47,090
And so as you can see there was
barely agreement or correlation

422
00:19:47,090 --> 00:19:48,570
among our outputs.

423
00:19:48,570 --> 00:19:51,590
And then, you know, when I
aggregated that and looked

424
00:19:51,590 --> 00:19:54,500
at how well do we correlate
with like, for example,

425
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:58,910
Surge and Scale, and so we
decided Scale AI, we had more--

426
00:19:58,910 --> 00:20:01,460
like, the maximum
overlap was with Scale

427
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:02,695
compared to like, say, Surge.

428
00:20:02,695 --> 00:20:05,590

429
00:20:05,590 --> 00:20:08,990
So we ended up collecting
20,000 dialogues,

430
00:20:08,990 --> 00:20:11,290
so we decided to
go with multi-turn.

431
00:20:11,290 --> 00:20:15,640
And because it was multi-turn,
you would have 20,000 overall

432
00:20:15,640 --> 00:20:18,120
dialogues, but the number
of prompts would be 80,000.

433
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:19,870
So there would be each
dialogue would have

434
00:20:19,870 --> 00:20:22,010
about four turns on an average.

435
00:20:22,010 --> 00:20:25,090
So a human would prompt it,
the model would respond.

436
00:20:25,090 --> 00:20:27,850
a human would read
the response, and then

437
00:20:27,850 --> 00:20:30,100
ask the follow up
question, and then

438
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:32,560
again, the model would
generate two responses,

439
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:35,080
and that is how it would go on.

440
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:37,630
And so the task distribution
we decided to follow

441
00:20:37,630 --> 00:20:40,900
was a little bit
different from what we

442
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:42,970
had for supervised fine-tuning.

443
00:20:42,970 --> 00:20:45,280
and the reason
behind that we wanted

444
00:20:45,280 --> 00:20:50,920
to focus more on tasks
that were like factual.

445
00:20:50,920 --> 00:20:53,170
Essentially, this
is more about making

446
00:20:53,170 --> 00:20:55,790
the model learn between
positive and negative signals.

447
00:20:55,790 --> 00:20:58,445
So making the model like
discriminate between like,

448
00:20:58,445 --> 00:21:00,070
you know, what is
factual, what is not,

449
00:21:00,070 --> 00:21:02,680
what is helpful, what is
not, and what is harmless

450
00:21:02,680 --> 00:21:03,880
and what is not.

451
00:21:03,880 --> 00:21:07,640
And for example, tasks
generation and brainstorming,

452
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:09,380
there's no one correct answer.

453
00:21:09,380 --> 00:21:12,780
Everyone can come up with
different lists or recipes,

454
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:14,960
and it's hard to say,
is this the best answer?

455
00:21:14,960 --> 00:21:16,350
Is it the most helpful answer?

456
00:21:16,350 --> 00:21:18,470
But if you ask a
factual question,

457
00:21:18,470 --> 00:21:21,770
it's very clear what is
correct and what is not.

458
00:21:21,770 --> 00:21:24,680
So that was kind of like our
reasoning behind doing this.

459
00:21:24,680 --> 00:21:26,210
And so this is a
task distribution

460
00:21:26,210 --> 00:21:28,670
that we came up with for
collecting the human preference

461
00:21:28,670 --> 00:21:31,380
dataset.

462
00:21:31,380 --> 00:21:33,210
Also about the
length, because we

463
00:21:33,210 --> 00:21:35,290
are doing this in a
multi-turn setting,

464
00:21:35,290 --> 00:21:38,610
and so we wanted to make sure
the entire dialogue could fit

465
00:21:38,610 --> 00:21:41,190
into the context
of the models, we

466
00:21:41,190 --> 00:21:44,790
decided to ask them to keep the
overall dialogue to be shorter

467
00:21:44,790 --> 00:21:48,090
than 2048 tokens.

468
00:21:48,090 --> 00:21:50,220
And then it was
multi-turn with an average

469
00:21:50,220 --> 00:21:52,410
of four turns per dialogue.

470
00:21:52,410 --> 00:21:54,390
Then obviously we
had to also select

471
00:21:54,390 --> 00:21:57,570
on the dimension of whether
we are going for helpful,

472
00:21:57,570 --> 00:22:00,810
or harmless or,
you know, honesty.

473
00:22:00,810 --> 00:22:04,360
So we followed this instructions
from this OpenAI guidelines.

474
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,130
I'm not sure if I
can pull this up.

475
00:22:06,130 --> 00:22:08,920
That would be nice.

476
00:22:08,920 --> 00:22:11,590
OK, great.

477
00:22:11,590 --> 00:22:15,520
But yeah, so OpenAI has this
document, which is public,

478
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:17,740
of labeling
instructions that they

479
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:20,008
shared with their annotators.

480
00:22:20,008 --> 00:22:22,550
And so they have-- obviously,
like I said, they have helpful,

481
00:22:22,550 --> 00:22:26,030
thoughtful, and harmless, but
then they also have this thing--

482
00:22:26,030 --> 00:22:27,010
how do I scroll down?

483
00:22:27,010 --> 00:22:28,405
OK.

484
00:22:28,405 --> 00:22:32,440
So they have definitions what do
they mean by helpfulness, what

485
00:22:32,440 --> 00:22:34,420
do they mean by truthfulness,
and what do they

486
00:22:34,420 --> 00:22:35,630
mean by harmlessness.

487
00:22:35,630 --> 00:22:39,190
So in our case, because our
models were not as good,

488
00:22:39,190 --> 00:22:42,380
we decided to focus on
helpfulness and truthfulness.

489
00:22:42,380 --> 00:22:44,770
And when they had to
break ties, OpenAI

