629
00:28:49,500 --> 00:28:52,860
OK, so I'm going to talk a
little bit about experiments

630
00:28:52,860 --> 00:28:55,870
and evaluation for
each of these recipes.

631
00:28:55,870 --> 00:28:59,280
One is collecting everything
with humans involved,

632
00:28:59,280 --> 00:29:02,370
and the second one is
everything, which is synthetic.

633
00:29:02,370 --> 00:29:04,500
But then before I
discuss evaluation,

634
00:29:04,500 --> 00:29:07,290
I wanted to talk about what
are the benchmarks that we

635
00:29:07,290 --> 00:29:09,690
are evaluating on and how
good are these benchmarks

636
00:29:09,690 --> 00:29:12,240
for evaluating chatbots?

637
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:13,800
And to think about
evaluation, we

638
00:29:13,800 --> 00:29:16,570
need to first think about how
are we training these models.

639
00:29:16,570 --> 00:29:19,260
So like today, all the
models that are trained

640
00:29:19,260 --> 00:29:21,970
are more or less have these
four ways of learning.

641
00:29:21,970 --> 00:29:25,080
The first one is pre-training
the language model, essentially

642
00:29:25,080 --> 00:29:27,750
predicting the next token,
and examples of these

643
00:29:27,750 --> 00:29:31,740
are like GPT-3, OPT, and so
like the foundation models.

644
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:34,770
The second type of learning
is incontext learning

645
00:29:34,770 --> 00:29:36,600
or the prompt-based learning.

646
00:29:36,600 --> 00:29:39,690
In this case, you're like
just giving a new kind of task

647
00:29:39,690 --> 00:29:42,420
in the context of the
model, and then you

648
00:29:42,420 --> 00:29:45,340
ask it to do that
on new examples.

649
00:29:45,340 --> 00:29:48,120
So if you wanted to write a
poem, for example, for GPT-3,

650
00:29:48,120 --> 00:29:50,440
you would have written
that in the context,

651
00:29:50,440 --> 00:29:55,110
and then it would have generated
new poem on some other topic.

652
00:29:55,110 --> 00:29:59,010
The third type of learning is
the supervised fine-tuning,

653
00:29:59,010 --> 00:30:02,500
which was kind of like the first
step of training a chatbot.

654
00:30:02,500 --> 00:30:05,460
But in this case, you're like
fine tuning on the instruction,

655
00:30:05,460 --> 00:30:08,110
following data, and then you
want these language models,

656
00:30:08,110 --> 00:30:09,810
which are just
pre-trained to predict

657
00:30:09,810 --> 00:30:12,750
the next token to become
chatty and to generate

658
00:30:12,750 --> 00:30:14,580
open ended responses.

659
00:30:14,580 --> 00:30:17,280
And then finally, the fourth
one is reinforcement learning

660
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:20,370
from human feedback, which
is nudging the language model

661
00:30:20,370 --> 00:30:21,820
towards the values you desire.

662
00:30:21,820 --> 00:30:25,030
And examples include
LLaMA2 chat from Meta.

663
00:30:25,030 --> 00:30:27,700

664
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:30,460
So the first two
steps are, you know,

665
00:30:30,460 --> 00:30:34,180
we have a lot of benchmarks for
these two types of training,

666
00:30:34,180 --> 00:30:37,810
like Sanford HELM is an example
of that or the Google BIG-bench

667
00:30:37,810 --> 00:30:41,290
or even Hugging Face
open LLM leaderboard.

668
00:30:41,290 --> 00:30:44,513
But for these two
types of learning,

669
00:30:44,513 --> 00:30:46,930
which is supervised fine-tuning
and reinforcement learning

670
00:30:46,930 --> 00:30:48,700
from human feedback,
which are parts

671
00:30:48,700 --> 00:30:52,390
of this recipe for
training a chatbot, not

672
00:30:52,390 --> 00:30:55,158
a lot of leaderboards or
evaluation benchmarks available,

673
00:30:55,158 --> 00:30:57,700
but there are some available,
and I wanted to like, you know,

674
00:30:57,700 --> 00:31:00,290
just highlight some of those.

675
00:31:00,290 --> 00:31:02,600
So like, yeah, this is
essentially like the steps

676
00:31:02,600 --> 00:31:03,360
three and four.

677
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:06,320
Your match to like, you know,
the step one over here, which

678
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:09,260
is helpfulness, and then steps
two and three over here, which

679
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:11,030
is like, you know,
nudging the model

680
00:31:11,030 --> 00:31:14,800
towards being more harmless.

681
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:19,570
So if you had to evaluate the
chatbot for each of these steps,

682
00:31:19,570 --> 00:31:21,280
you would have to
think about, how

683
00:31:21,280 --> 00:31:24,180
do you evaluate instruction
following a chattiness?

684
00:31:24,180 --> 00:31:26,050
You would have to
think about how do you

685
00:31:26,050 --> 00:31:29,240
evaluate the reward model, which
is essentially a classifier?

686
00:31:29,240 --> 00:31:31,360
And then finally
think about, how

687
00:31:31,360 --> 00:31:32,920
do you evaluate
for harmlessness,

688
00:31:32,920 --> 00:31:34,990
which is by red
teaming or adversary

689
00:31:34,990 --> 00:31:38,290
prompting the language model?

690
00:31:38,290 --> 00:31:40,450
So for the first step, you
would have to see like,

691
00:31:40,450 --> 00:31:43,090
does the model generate
useful responses on the topic,

692
00:31:43,090 --> 00:31:44,270
and are they open ended?

693
00:31:44,270 --> 00:31:46,330
And one example of
a prompt that you

694
00:31:46,330 --> 00:31:47,950
would try to evaluate
the model would

695
00:31:47,950 --> 00:31:50,560
be to like, say, brainstorm
a list of the New Year's

696
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:52,300
resolution.

697
00:31:52,300 --> 00:31:56,320
And so examples of benchmarks
and evaluation boards

698
00:31:56,320 --> 00:31:59,980
that are looking at this sort
of supervised fine-tuning

699
00:31:59,980 --> 00:32:03,650
is like Hugging Face's
leaderboard with Elo ratings.

700
00:32:03,650 --> 00:32:05,860
So Elo is this
metric that is used

701
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:07,960
