00:44:59,640 --> 00:45:03,340
And then that as well we found
that wasn't very helpful.

979
00:45:03,340 --> 00:45:07,150
So the best recipe,
obviously, is DPO plus SFT.

980
00:45:07,150 --> 00:45:10,770
So, you know, doing SFT first
on the UltraChat, and then

981
00:45:10,770 --> 00:45:12,240
DPO on the UltraFeedfback.

982
00:45:12,240 --> 00:45:15,840
Both of these data
sets are synthetic,

983
00:45:15,840 --> 00:45:18,890
but it's like only slightly
better than just doing SFT.

984
00:45:18,890 --> 00:45:21,660

985
00:45:21,660 --> 00:45:24,870
OK, so I'm getting to this
final section of my talk, which

986
00:45:24,870 --> 00:45:27,450
is essentially looking at--

987
00:45:27,450 --> 00:45:30,570
so we have seen a lot of these
evaluation and benchmarks

988
00:45:30,570 --> 00:45:32,520
and leaderboards,
and many of them

989
00:45:32,520 --> 00:45:35,400
are starting to adopt these
powerful models like Claude

990
00:45:35,400 --> 00:45:40,090
2 and GPT-4, and are using as
proxy for humans in evaluation.

991
00:45:40,090 --> 00:45:42,610
And so what are the quirks
associated with doing that,

992
00:45:42,610 --> 00:45:44,068
and are there things
that we should

993
00:45:44,068 --> 00:45:46,320
like be considering
when we are doing

994
00:45:46,320 --> 00:45:49,190
this at a very large scale?

995
00:45:49,190 --> 00:45:52,790
So when we did that, when we
use GPT-4 as an evaluator,

996
00:45:52,790 --> 00:45:56,070
we found that it actually
has a positional bias.

997
00:45:56,070 --> 00:45:59,150
And so in particular, it is
predisposed to generating

998
00:45:59,150 --> 00:46:02,820
a rating of one in a pair-wise
preference collection setting.

999
00:46:02,820 --> 00:46:05,090
And so this chart
over here shows

1000
00:46:05,090 --> 00:46:09,110
like the average rating
of four model responses

1001
00:46:09,110 --> 00:46:11,000
across the entire dataset.

1002
00:46:11,000 --> 00:46:12,710
And on the right,
on the other hand,

1003
00:46:12,710 --> 00:46:14,670
humans are more or less uniform.

1004
00:46:14,670 --> 00:46:17,832
And so you expect that this
distribution seems much more

1005
00:46:17,832 --> 00:46:20,290
better than this distribution
which is skewed to the right.

1006
00:46:20,290 --> 00:46:22,990

1007
00:46:22,990 --> 00:46:25,480
So then what we did
is that we prompted

1008
00:46:25,480 --> 00:46:28,450
GPT-4 to say that, hey,
you have this left bias,

1009
00:46:28,450 --> 00:46:31,040
and you always generate
this rating of one.

1010
00:46:31,040 --> 00:46:33,100
You know, be aware of
this bias, and then

1011
00:46:33,100 --> 00:46:35,710
you tell it to de-bias
itself, it actually

1012
00:46:35,710 --> 00:46:37,970
flips the bias in the
opposite direction.

1013
00:46:37,970 --> 00:46:41,233
So then it is more
self-aware in the sense

1014
00:46:41,233 --> 00:46:43,150
that it knows that, you
know, it has this bias

1015
00:46:43,150 --> 00:46:46,370
and now it starts generating
more ratings of five and six.

1016
00:46:46,370 --> 00:46:48,370
And the one way of
getting rid of this

1017
00:46:48,370 --> 00:46:52,030
is that we make sure that each
response is equally likely to be

1018
00:46:52,030 --> 00:46:54,790
in right and left position
so that kind of dilutes

1019
00:46:54,790 --> 00:47:00,060
like this bias that it has
to each of these positions.

1020
00:47:00,060 --> 00:47:03,330
And then, you know, we found
that actually like prompting

1021
00:47:03,330 --> 00:47:06,300
GPT-4 to generate scores--
so asking it to score

1022
00:47:06,300 --> 00:47:09,010
each response individually
like MT bench does.

1023
00:47:09,010 --> 00:47:11,760
And then instead of ranking
it in a pair-wise setting,

1024
00:47:11,760 --> 00:47:13,410
we actually found
that alleviates

1025
00:47:13,410 --> 00:47:16,320
the problem a little bit,
but does not completely

1026
00:47:16,320 --> 00:47:18,690
get rid of the problem.

1027
00:47:18,690 --> 00:47:23,230
We also found evidence of doping
between training and evaluation.

1028
00:47:23,230 --> 00:47:27,600
So in particular, we found
that GPT-4 prefers models that

1029
00:47:27,600 --> 00:47:30,070
were trained on GPT-4's data.

1030
00:47:30,070 --> 00:47:32,370
So all these models
here were trained

1031
00:47:32,370 --> 00:47:36,360
on data that was
bootstrapped using GPT-4.

1032
00:47:36,360 --> 00:47:39,420
So it prefers that
over humans who

1033
00:47:39,420 --> 00:47:42,210
are more factual, much
more higher quality,

1034
00:47:42,210 --> 00:47:45,270
but they might be very
succinct and to the point.

1035
00:47:45,270 --> 00:47:47,280
So this is one
thing that we should

1036
00:47:47,280 --> 00:47:50,880
be aware of when we're
using GPT-4 as an evaluator.

1037
00:47:50,880 --> 00:47:54,450
The other thing is that it's
also like concurs with findings

1038
00:47:54,450 --> 00:47:58,050
from these other papers, which
is that GPT-4 prefers models

1039
00:47:58,050 --> 00:47:59,630
with higher diversity.

1040
00:47:59,630 --> 00:48:02,830
So that is number of unique
tokens in the response

1041
00:48:02,830 --> 00:48:04,550
and the longer responses.

1042
00:48:04,550 --> 00:48:06,760
So if you have this list
of list kind of response,

1043
00:48:06,760 --> 00:48:10,990
just like ChatGPT does,
GPT-4 is predisposed

1044
00:48:10,990 --> 00:48:13,030
to rating that higher
compared to a model that

1045
00:48:13,030 --> 00:48:14,130
does not generate that.

1046
00:48:14,130 --> 00:48:17,690

1047
00:48:17,690 --> 00:48:21,410
We also found that GPT-4 has
poor correlation with humans

1048
00:48:21,410 --> 00:48:24,490
on low entropy tasks such as
math, coding, and reasoning.

1049
00:48:24,490 --> 00:48:26,420
So remember that a
leaderboard I showed you

1050
00:48:26,420 --> 00:48:30,050
where we had compared like how
does GPT-4 Elo rating compare

1051
00:48:30,050 --> 00:48:33,620
to humans, and then we dive
deeper into how does that

1052
00:48:33,620 --> 00:48:36,230
compare on each of these
different tasks, distribution

1053
00:48:36,230 --> 00:48:37,103
and categories?

1054
00:48:37,103 --> 00:48:38,520
And so this is
what it looks like.

1055
00:48:38,520 --> 00:48:42,620
So it seems like it says
lower correlation with humans

1056
00:48:42,620 --> 00:48:45,530
on some of these more
factual, like, you know,

1057
00:48:45,530 --> 00:48:47,970
kind of expecting
one correct answer,

1058
00:48:47,970 --> 00:48:50,900
and they're actually highly
correlated with humans

1059
00:48:50,900 --> 00:48:53,568
on these more high
entropy tasks where you're

1060
00:48:53,568 --> 00:48:55,610
like brainstorming and
creative generation, which

1061
00:48:55,610 --> 00:48:58,250
was kind of unintuitive
and counterintuitive

1062
00:48:58,250 --> 00:49:02,030
because you could have so many
different ways of coming up

1063
00:49:02,030 --> 00:49:04,970
with a recipe or a
list of something.

1064
00:49:04,970 --> 00:49:07,910
But that's where the
rating of GPT-4 and humans

1065
00:49:07,910 --> 00:49:09,180
are more correlated.

1066
00:49:09,180 --> 00:49:12,100

1067
00:49:12,100 --> 00:49:15,140
OK, so the final
thing is takeaways.

1068
00:49:15,140 --> 00:49:18,250
So there's a bunch of this,
but let's try to break it down.

1069
00:49:18,250 --> 00:49:21,340
Essentially, we discussed
how do we come up

1070
00:49:21,340 --> 00:49:25,800
with steps for data curation for
supervised fine-tuning and RLHF,

1071
00:49:25,800 --> 00:49:29,560
and it involves several critical
factors such as how much data

1072
00:49:29,560 --> 00:49:33,070
do you need to collect, what
is the length of the prompts

1073
00:49:33,070 --> 00:49:36,170
and the distribution of those
length, the task distribution,

1074
00:49:36,170 --> 00:49:37,810
and what is the role of humans?

1075
00:49:37,810 --> 00:49:39,470
Like, do you need
synthetic data?

1076
00:49:39,470 --> 00:49:41,920
Do you need completely
manually curated or something

1077
00:49:41,920 --> 00:49:42,945
in the middle?

1078
00:49:42,945 --> 00:49:44,320
And we looked at,
like, there are

1079
00:49:44,320 --> 00:49:48,490
many tools for efficient
fine-tuning of open source LLMs.

1080
00:49:48,490 --> 00:49:52,690
From the SFT results,
we found that truthfulqa

1081
00:49:52,690 --> 00:49:54,580
was the main
differentiating benchmark

1082
00:49:54,580 --> 00:49:57,340
for these automated
eval metrics.

1083
00:49:57,340 --> 00:50:00,310
And then we found that MT
bench scores were actually not

1084
00:50:00,310 --> 00:50:02,660
correlated with these
automated metrics,

1085
00:50:02,660 --> 00:50:06,010
and so it was more sort of--

1086
00:50:06,010 --> 00:50:10,150
only on some of these models we
found that they were correlated.

1087
00:50:10,150 --> 00:50:11,960
For the distillation
results, which

1088
00:50:11,960 --> 00:50:14,960
is from the Zephyr-7B where
we are like fine-tuning

1089
00:50:14,960 --> 00:50:18,920
on synthetic data, we
found that the SFT on AI

1090
00:50:18,920 --> 00:50:22,310
generated data and the
DPO or distillation of DPO

1091
00:50:22,310 --> 00:50:25,250
on AI feedback data
actually beats ChatGPT

1092
00:50:25,250 --> 00:50:28,550
even though the model is
just 7 billion parameter.

1093
00:50:28,550 --> 00:50:31,130
And then we found
that benchmarking gap

1094
00:50:31,130 --> 00:50:33,590
in assessing our RLHF
models, in particular,

1095
00:50:33,590 --> 00:50:38,340
that we don't have benchmarks
for assessing reward models,

1096
00:50:38,340 --> 00:50:40,520
and we also don't have
open source benchmarks

1097
00:50:40,520 --> 00:50:44,450
for evaluating red-teaming
and model vulnerabilities.

1098
00:50:44,450 --> 00:50:48,080
Then finally, we'd dive
deeper into looking

1099
00:50:48,080 --> 00:50:51,770
at quirks of using GPT-4 or
some of these powerful LLMs

1100
00:50:51,770 --> 00:50:54,530
as an evaluator, and
some of them were like,

1101
00:50:54,530 --> 00:50:57,930
you know, they prefer models
trained on GPT-4 like data,

1102
00:50:57,930 --> 00:51:00,710
it has a left positional
bias, and then

1103
00:51:00,710 --> 00:51:02,690
it has high
correlation with humans

1104
00:51:02,690 --> 00:51:07,960
on creative tasks compared
to coding or reasoning tasks.

